          "text": "**Observations**\n\n- *Spatial layout of W1.* The very first lines (image row 0, columns ~7–21) and the first lines of row 1 already carry weights of 0.15–0.42 (e.g. (0,10)=0.161/0.179, (0,11)=0.214, (0,13)=−0.276, (1,10)=0.338, (1,17)=0.424/0.325, (1,18)=0.283/0.267). Likewise the last image row 27 has large weights at columns 13–14 (≈ −0.25 in units 10, 11, 12; +0.22/+0.24 in unit 6) and at columns 19–23 (+0.21 to +0.22 in unit 12, +0.18 in unit 15). Only the extreme corners and the outermost few columns (cols 0–4 and 23–27) stay at pure initialisation noise (|w| ≲ 0.04). So the training images contain ink that reaches the top and bottom edges in the central columns; the objects fill the full 28‑pixel height but are narrower horizontally.\n- *Hidden units.* Several units are \"top‑edge/bottom‑edge\" detectors: unit 10 (bias −1.07, i.e. off by default) is strongly positive on the top‑centre band and strongly negative at the bottom centre (27,13–14) – it fires for an object that is solid at the top but has a gap at the bottom centre. Unit 6 is the opposite sign at the bottom centre (positive where the bottom middle is bright). Unit 13 is positive at the top \"shoulder\" positions (cols 10–11, 17) and negative at the top centre (cols 13–15) – a neckline‑shaped template. Units 2, 5, 11 have broad horizontal bands of weight in the middle/lower part of the image rather than tall templates. Overall the weights are smooth, blob/band‑like templates, not thin stroke detectors.\n- *Output layer.* W2 shows clear class clusters. Unit 2: +0.95/+1.02/+0.97 for classes 5, 7, 9 and −1.30 for class 8, −0.66 for class 2. Unit 5: + for 5, 7, 9 (and 2), − for 0, 1, 3, 8. Unit 11: + for 5, 7, 8, 9, − for 0, 2, 3, 6. Unit 9: + for 2, 3, 4, 6, − for 5, 7, 8, 9. So classes {5, 7, 9} behave as one visual family, {0, 2, 3, 4, 6} as another, class 1 and class 8 are each somewhat isolated. Unit 10 (the \"solid top / gap at bottom centre\" unit) feeds +0.64 to class 1 and −1.46 to class 5, −0.93 to class 0; unit 6 (bright bottom centre) gives −1.17 to class 1. Unit 13 (neckline template) gives +0.98 to class 0 and −1.16 to class 4.\n\n**Reasoning**\n\n- MNIST‑style digit datasets (MNIST, EMNIST, KMNIST) centre the glyph in a 20×20 box with a 4‑pixel blank border, so rows 0–3 and 24–27 are never non‑zero and their weights would remain at initialisation noise. Here rows 0, 1 and 27 carry some of the largest weights in the whole matrix – ruled out. The templates are also broad bands/blobs, not stroke‑oriented.\n- Objects that span the full height in the centre columns, with a frequently dark centre at the bottom row (gap between two legs) opposed to a solid top band (waistband), and a top‑centre notch between two bright \"shoulders\" (neckline) point to clothing silhouettes.\n- The output structure matches Fashion‑MNIST's label order exactly: the {5, 7, 9} footwear family (sandal, sneaker, ankle boot – wide, low, horizontally oriented) shares units; the {0, 2, 3, 4, 6} upper‑body/dress family shares units; class 1 (trouser) is driven by the \"solid top, gap at bottom centre\" unit and suppressed by the \"bottom centre filled\" unit; class 8 (bag) is anti‑correlated with the shoe units; the neckline unit favours class 0 (T‑shirt) over class 4 (coat).\n\n**Dataset**\n\nFashion‑MNIST (Zalando article images, 28×28 grayscale, 10 classes).\n\n**Classes**\n\n0 T‑shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence**\n\n85%",
